WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.982
Compare two saved economies.

2
00:00:01.982 --> 00:00:07.285
We change sigma, keeping zeta, potential
firms, and the underlying shocks the same.

3
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:14.135
These fourteen firms belong to one
illustrated market.

4
00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:16.028
Keep their identities fixed.

5
00:00:16.028 --> 00:00:19.340
Increasing sigma spreads their
productivities farther apart.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.342
The model solves market shares and
markups, then applies its exit rule.

7
00:00:29.342 --> 00:00:32.255
These lower bars show final sales shares.

8
00:00:32.255 --> 00:00:33.503
Zero means inactive.

9
00:00:33.503 --> 00:00:35.583
The bars describe this market;

10
00:00:35.583 --> 00:00:37.663
they are not economy-wide weights.

11
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.691
Across all ten thousand markets, wages and
output adjust with firm decisions.

12
00:00:45.691 --> 00:00:51.477
In this saved comparison, output rises,
wages rise, and fewer firms remain active.

13
00:00:51.477 --> 00:00:55.483
These economy-wide numbers come from the
full saved solution.

14
00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.377
The solution gives three estimation
moments.

15
00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:03.413
In this comparison, employment
reallocation rises, the sales-weighted

16
00:01:03.413 --> 00:01:07.954
markup rises, and the sales-weighted
overhead cost share falls.

17
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.102
Reallocation uses employment in both
periods.

18
00:01:15.102 --> 00:01:18.313
Markup and overhead use first-period sales
weights.

19
00:01:18.313 --> 00:01:23.358
The overhead moment averages firm cost
ratios, not economy-wide cost totals.
